				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">caelator,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Juv.
					9, 145</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vel gibberosi vel curvi,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Dig.
					21, 1, 3</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>crooked, wrong,
				perverse</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mores,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Pers.
					3, 52</span></i>
			.—<i>Subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">curvum</span></b> , i, n., <i>that which is crooked</i> or <i>wrong</i> (opp. rectum): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">scilicet ut possem curvo dignoscere rectum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Hor. Ep. 2, 2, 44</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">rectum discernis, ubi inter Curva subit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Pers.
					4, 12</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">invenimus qui curva corrigeret,</span>”
				<i>set every thing right</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					Ep. 5, 8</span></i>
			 (21), 6: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hic nobis curva corriget?</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen.
					Apoc. 8 <i>fin.</i></span></i>
			</div>
